    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.4",
    "topic_title": "Photosynthesis",
    "stem": "Isolated chloroplasts are placed in a buffer that contains water but no CO₂ and are then illuminated with bright light. Which outcome is expected during the first few minutes of illumination?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "No O₂ is released, because the O₂ produced in photosynthesis comes from the oxygen atoms in CO₂.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap assumes O₂ comes from CO₂ because both molecules contain oxygen. In fact O₂ is released when water is split to replace electrons lost at photosystem II, so O₂ release continues without CO₂. Fix: Trace each product to its stage and ask which input molecule is split in the thylakoid to release O₂."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Sugar is still produced in the stroma, because light supplies the energy the Calvin cycle needs to build carbohydrates.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap confuses an energy source with a carbon source. Light reactions supply ATP and NADPH, but the Calvin cycle needs CO₂ as the carbon atoms of the sugar, so no carbohydrate forms without it. Fix: For any product, list both the energy input and the matter input, and check that each is present."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "ATP and NADPH production stops immediately, because the light reactions need CO₂ as their source of electrons.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap assigns CO₂ a role in the light reactions. The electrons that flow from photosystem II through the ETC to reduce NADP⁺ at photosystem I come from water, not CO₂, so ATP and NADPH still form in light. Fix: Ask which molecule donates electrons to photosystem II before deciding whether a missing input halts the light reactions."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Water is split and O₂ is released at the thylakoids, but no sugar is produced in the stroma.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The light reactions in the thylakoid membranes need only light and water. Water splitting replaces electrons lost at photosystem II and releases O₂, and the light reactions also make ATP and NADPH. The Calvin cycle in the stroma builds carbohydrate from CO₂, so without CO₂ no sugar is made even though ATP and NADPH are available."
     }
    ]
   },
