   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.4",
    "topic_title": "Photosynthesis",
    "stem": "A student is building a model of photosynthesis in an illuminated chloroplast. Which statement correctly connects the locations, inputs, products, and energy transfer of the two major stages?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Light reactions in the stroma use light energy to produce ATP and NADPH, releasing O₂ from water. The Calvin cycle at the thylakoid membranes uses ATP and NADPH to build carbohydrates from CO₂.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Location-swap error: this reverses the sites of the two stages; light reactions occur at thylakoid membranes, while the Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma. Fix: Draw a chloroplast and place each stage beside its correct compartment before connecting their products."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Light reactions at the thylakoid membranes use light energy to produce ATP and NADPH. The Calvin cycle in the stroma uses ATP and NADPH to build carbohydrates from CO₂, releasing O₂ from CO₂.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Oxygen-source confusion: this treats carbon fixation as the source of released oxygen, but photosynthetic O₂ comes from water splitting during the light reactions. Fix: Trace water to oxygen release and CO₂ to carbohydrate production separately in your model."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Light reactions at the thylakoid membranes use light energy to produce ATP and NADPH, releasing O₂ from water. The Calvin cycle in the stroma uses ATP and NADPH to build carbohydrates from CO₂.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "This links energy capture to carbon fixation: the light reactions convert light energy into chemical energy in ATP and NADPH, which power carbohydrate production in the Calvin cycle. It also correctly assigns oxygen release to water splitting rather than carbon fixation."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Light reactions at the thylakoid membranes use light energy to build carbohydrates from CO₂, releasing O₂ from water. The Calvin cycle in the stroma produces the ATP and NADPH needed by the light reactions.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Stage-product reversal: this assigns carbohydrate production to the light reactions and ATP/NADPH production to the Calvin cycle, reversing their roles and energy dependency. Fix: Draw arrows carrying ATP and NADPH from the light reactions to the Calvin cycle, then identify where CO₂ enters."
     }
    ]
   },
